-### The three modifier bits, and they sit in a two-layer pair
-
-```c
-if ((recipient[0x6B] & 0x08) || (recipient[0x7B] & 0x08))  EXP = EXP * 2;
-if ((recipient[0x6B] & 0x20) || (recipient[0x7B] & 0x20))  EXP = 0;
-if ((recipient[0x6B] & 0x10) || (recipient[0x7B] & 0x10))  LP  = LP  * 2;
-```
-
-| Bit | Effect |
-|---|---|
-| `0x08` | **double EXP** |
-| `0x10` | **double LP** |
-| `0x20` | **zero EXP** |
-
-**Three separate effects, three separate bits, one byte** — and each is tested in **two parallel
-bytes at `+0x6B` and `+0x7B`**, the same bit position in both. That is the convention documented in
-[`../10-Status-Periodic-Terminal/Status-And-Augment-Layers/two-layer-effect-model.md`](../10-Status-Periodic-Terminal/Status-And-Augment-Layers/two-layer-effect-model.md):
-an effect is set in either of two layers and the engine tests both. This is an **eleventh
-independent sighting**, and the first at this offset pair — the status masks use `+0x3C`/`+0x64`,
-these use `+0x6B`/`+0x7B`, so the pattern is a *convention applied at several field pairs* rather
-than one pair of fields.
